{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186119936 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a" :question/id #uuid "e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71033" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(1,0,-2),\,E(-2,-2,5)$, and $F(-2,4,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\overset{\to }{DE}$ and $\overset{\to }{DF},$ as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\overset{\to }{DE} & =⟨-2,-2,5⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\  & =⟨-3,-2,7⟩ \\ \overset{\to }{DF} & =⟨-2,4,3⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\  & =⟨-3,4,5⟩\end{aligned}
$$

Now, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\overset{\to }{DE}+t\overset{\to }{DF} \\ r & =⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\,s,t\in (-\infty ,\infty ).\end{aligned}
